@page
@{
	ViewData["Title"] = "Mitsubishi";
	ViewData["Description"] = "The history of Mitsubishi: how the Japanese conglomerate with the three diamonds became a car manufacturer.";
	Layout = "~/Pages/Shared/_Layout.cshtml";
}

<div class="text-center">
	<h1>Mitsubishi - The Mother of a Legend</h1>
	<figure class="figure">
		<img src="~/images/mitsubishi/Iwasaki.jpg" alt="Iataro Iwasaki" class="img-thumbnail" />
		<figcaption>Iataro Iwasaki</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<p>
		In 1870, a young graduate of the imperial college bought three merchant ships
		and thereby opened up the paths to global trade relations; his name was Yataro
		Iwasaki. When he founded his company, he had no inkling of the off-road vehicle
		that would later take the hearts of off-road drivers by storm. Only 5 years after
		his first investment, Yataro Iwasaki was the head of a company
		based in Tokyo that was viewed with favour by the imperial court and awarded government
		contracts. It was also during these years that the company name Mitsubishi came into being, which
		translated from Japanese means three diamonds. Today you will find them as the symbol
		on every motor vehicle of the Mitsubishi group.
	</p>
	<figure class="figure">
		<img src="~/images/mitsubishi/KitaBisamBridge.jpg" class="img-thumbnail" alt="Kita Bisam Bridge" />
		<figcaption>Kita Bisam Bridge</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<p>
		As the company's history went on, its field of activity fanned
		out further and further from its actual core business, namely trade, and so today the
		Tokio Marine and Fire Insurance as well as the
		Mitsubishi Trust and Banking Corporation belong to the Mitsubishi group. The generational change
		at the top of the company cleared the way for a new outlook, for the
		son of the company founder, Koyata Iwasaki, was regarded as a modern-thinking, European-influenced
		man who had studied at the famous Cambridge University in England. He drove the
		internationalisation of the Mitsubishi group further forward, and so today companies
		such as the Mitsubishi Oil Company or Mitsubishi Heavy Industries — an armaments firm
		whose machines milled the Eurotunnel between England and France through the seabed —
		but also the camera manufacturer Nippon Kogaku K.K., better known by
		the name Nikon, can be found in Mitsubishi's portfolio. Mitsubishi's range of activities
		covers every conceivable area of industry, from bridge and road construction
		through microelectronics to shipyards and space-shuttle yards. In the field of
		microelectronics, Mitsubishi is among the ten largest companies worldwide, the shipbuilding division
		is planning the construction of the fastest transport ship at 180 km/h, and in 1994 the
		first rocket developed under Mitsubishi's direction, the H2, set off on its flight into space.
	</p>

	<figure class="figure">
		<img src="~/images/mitsubishi/ModelA.jpg" class="img-thumbnail" alt="The Model A" />
		<figcaption>The Model A</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<p>
		It was not until the penultimate year of the First World War, 47 years after the company's founding,
		that Mitsubishi entered automobile manufacturing with an exact copy of the Italian Fiat Tipo Zero.
		In Japan this car was offered under the name Model A.
		In 1918 the automotive branch of the Mitsubishi group shifted its focus to the
		commercial vehicle sector — research and development were pushed forward, and so
		the company can claim a number of successes today, such as the
		development of the first Japanese diesel engine (1931), or, a year later, the
		production of a 100 hp petrol engine that made the B46 the fastest bus in Nippon.
	</p>
	<figure class="figure">
		<img src="~/images/mitsubishi/Nikon.jpg" class="img-thumbnail" alt="Nikon" />
		<figcaption>Nikon</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<p>
		In 1934 the first diesel-powered four-wheel-drive vehicle, the PX-33, made its debut. Japan's involvement
		in the Second World War forced Mitsubishi, as a high-tech group, to manufacture armoured vehicles
		and aircraft, and it ended with the complete destruction of the production facilities
		at the end of the war. Even today, with Mitsubishi Heavy Industries, the company has
		a firm foothold in the armaments sector. The subsidiary ranks among the twenty largest
		arms suppliers in the world. In 1950 Mitsubishi's automotive branch reported its first successes again
		with the development of the Fuso R1, Japan's first front-wheel-drive bus, and, a
		year later, the T31, which with a payload of 4 tonnes was Japan's heaviest truck.
	</p>
	<p>
		It was not until 1959 that Mitsubishi returned to its old strength and devoted itself once more
		to the passenger car. That year saw the first truck with a tilting cab
		and a four-seat small car with an air-cooled 21 hp half-litre engine. Three years
		later Mitsubishi took up arms and launched the Colt, a small car
		that even today remains a fixture in the small-car segment.
		The first Mitsubishis did not appear in Germany until the founding of MMC, the
		Mitsubishi Motor Corporation Deutschland GmbH, in 1977, and they have been multiplying
		rapidly since 1982. In that year MMC presented a veritable flood of new models,
		among them the L300 four-wheel-drive van, the compact Lancer F, the Tredia as a mid-size car
		and the Cordia as a sports coupé. In addition, the off-road vehicle christened
		Pajero also appeared for the first time — the car that was to take Germany by storm.
	</p>
	<p>
		In 1989 sales reached half a million, and six years later the
		counter leapt past the magic mark of one million Mitsubishi passenger cars sold.
	</p>
</div>
